-- `@[trusted]` rescues an undischarged POSTCONDITION under `cap verified`,
-- the same way it already rescued an undischarged precondition (Task 2 of
-- the 2026-07-30 refinement backlog). Without this, Task 3's new escalation
-- would make `cap verified` LESS adoptable: a function with a genuinely
-- undecidable return contract would have no way to opt in short of dropping
-- the capability for the whole module.
--
-- `mk` is identical to the reject companion
-- `reject/t134_refine_postcondition_strict_undischarged` -- same signature,
-- same body (`z * z + 1`: nonlinear, so unreflectable; TRUE on every input,
-- so the 2026-08-30 witness battery cannot refute it either), same
-- undecidable obligation -- except `mk` now carries `@[trusted]`. The
-- postcondition is still SKIPPED (the checker still cannot decide it), but
-- that skip is now recorded as `Trusted` -- an assertion, not a proof --
-- and the escalation is suppressed.  (`@[trusted]` rescues SKIPS only: a
-- witness-CONFIRMED violation is never rescued.)
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md, "`@[trusted]` — a scoped, loud escape
-- hatch".
mod T135 do

  cap verified

  @[trusted]
  fn mk(z : Int) : {Int | _ > 0} do
    z * z + 1
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    mk(1)
  end

end
